WEBVTT


00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:07.320
Expand your horizons with your LK-150
knitting machine by using skinny yarn.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:10.969
Use sock yarn on your LK-150?

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:11.970
Sure!

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:17.080
You might be surprised at the results you
can get by using different yarns with your

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:17.760
machine.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:23.160
Hi, I'm Sue, the voice and the
hands behind the Knit it Now videos.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:28.740
For over 10 years, we've been helping
knitters just like you learn more about

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:29.670
their knitting machines

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:33.720
and yarn and have success
with their machine knitting.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:40.140
Let's take a look as we play with some
skinny yarn and get some surprising

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:40.740
results.

00:00:43.004 --> 00:00:49.275
The terms used to describe yarn weights
are really subjective and regional, but

00:00:49.275 --> 00:00:53.205
let's use the craft yarn counsel's
numbered standards for today's

00:00:53.205 --> 00:00:54.105
conversation.

00:00:55.425 --> 00:01:02.165
Often LK-150 knitters use worsted weight
yarn, a medium or a number 4 yarn.

00:01:02.835 --> 00:01:07.755
This is a very common weight used by hand
knitters, and it's part of the appeal of

00:01:07.755 --> 00:01:09.055
the LK-150.

00:01:09.405 --> 00:01:13.664
Knitters who are new to machine
knitting, are comfortable with this yarn.

00:01:14.205 --> 00:01:18.645
It hand knits on a size 7 or
9 US hand knitting needle.

00:01:20.325 --> 00:01:26.025
Now, depending on the yarn, machine
knitters would knit this at a tension 6 to

00:01:26.025 --> 00:01:28.545
8 on the LK-150.

00:01:30.585 --> 00:01:32.355
Now something to keep in mind.

00:01:32.745 --> 00:01:37.395
Again, these terms and these
categories are very subjective.

00:01:38.085 --> 00:01:41.495
A lot depends on the fiber
and the twist of the yarn.

00:01:42.765 --> 00:01:47.985
These two yarns are categorized as a
4 or a medium, but they ended up very

00:01:47.985 --> 00:01:48.765
differently.

00:01:49.155 --> 00:01:52.185
One is more loosely
twisted than the other.

00:01:56.565 --> 00:02:00.705
With that being said, have you tried
a lighter weight yarn on your machine?

00:02:01.455 --> 00:02:05.265
There are very few hard and
fast rules in machine knitting.

00:02:06.315 --> 00:02:08.234
But there are a few things to consider.

00:02:10.755 --> 00:02:16.305
I'm going to knit a get-acquainted swatch
and put my machine through its paces with

00:02:16.305 --> 00:02:17.095
this yarn.

00:02:17.805 --> 00:02:20.715
I bet you're asking what's
a get-acquainted swatch?

00:02:21.495 --> 00:02:23.565
It's a way of determining.

00:02:24.179 --> 00:02:27.179
What tension you like to knit a yarn at?

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:33.029
What are the results at a specific tension
with a specific yarn that you like?

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:39.630
This is a skill that you need to learn
to play with different yarns and your

00:02:39.649 --> 00:02:40.399
knitting machine.

00:02:42.079 --> 00:02:44.179
First, consider your mask tension.

00:02:45.405 --> 00:02:51.105
The yarn I'm using is Yeoman's Sport and
it's actually quite fine, and I would

00:02:51.105 --> 00:02:53.685
consider it more of a sock weight yarn.

00:02:54.255 --> 00:03:00.915
So there is that terminology again,
potato, potatoe, Sock Weight, DK, four

00:03:00.915 --> 00:03:02.304
-ply, eight-ply.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:09.749
When I thread up my mast, I immediately
noticed that said, at tension 5.

00:03:10.049 --> 00:03:14.310
The yarn was slack and the take-up
spring was standing straight up.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:18.400
It wasn't doing its job
of tensioning the yarn.

00:03:18.929 --> 00:03:25.320
I turned the dial down to 1, I tightened
it all the way and this feels better as I

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.660
thread up my carriage
and get started knitting.

00:03:29.519 --> 00:03:32.339
The next thing to consider
is the carriage tension.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.100
This controls the stitch size.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:41.100
I went ahead and cast on at about a
tension 3 so I could just easily get some

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:42.210
stitches on the needles.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:46.370
But I'll start knitting
my swatch at tension 1.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:48.060
Knit a few rows.

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:50.820
The machine is knitting the stitches well.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.890
No loops or struggles.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:54.960
The carriage isn't hard to push.

00:03:55.980 --> 00:04:02.310
So I know after that first row or two,
that tension 1 is possible and I'll

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:08.490
continue knitting a few inches, but I
can tell right away that the knitting is

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:09.660
really tight.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:17.190
Moving up to tension 2, I'll knit a few
inches and the knitting that the fabric is

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.470
starting to feel a whole lot better.

00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:25.960
Move up to tension 3 and
I'm really liking tension 3.

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:32.810
Tension 4 started to get sloppy
and not what I would want.

00:04:33.795 --> 00:04:36.315
Now, this is again, very, very subjective.

00:04:36.315 --> 00:04:43.065
You may love this yarn attention 4 or
5 and it would create a beautiful, very

00:04:43.065 --> 00:04:44.535
drapey fabric.

00:04:45.255 --> 00:04:51.455
But for the project I have in mind,
tension 4 or 5 probably won't work for me.

00:04:52.295 --> 00:04:56.720
After I dropped this, get acquainted
swatch off the machine, I dressed it,

00:04:57.140 --> 00:05:02.690
which means I washed it steamed, it, did
everything to it that I will do to my

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:03.890
final garment,

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:10.730
and then this now will be the final
test to determine what I really like.

00:05:11.625 --> 00:05:17.055
Now that I've determined what I like, the
next step is to knit a proper gauge swatch

00:05:17.205 --> 00:05:20.585
and establish the gauge for
my project with this yarn.

00:05:20.865 --> 00:05:23.115
I'm not going to use this
get acquainted swatch.

00:05:23.265 --> 00:05:27.945
I need to knit a proper gauge swatch
that I can get an accurate gauge.

00:05:30.185 --> 00:05:34.685
The bottom line behind this whole
experiment, can I use skinny yarns on

00:05:34.685 --> 00:05:36.155
LK-150?

00:05:36.665 --> 00:05:37.295
Yes.

00:05:37.995 --> 00:05:39.795
Well, I'd be happy with the results.

00:05:39.885 --> 00:05:41.025
Absolutely.

00:05:41.565 --> 00:05:47.245
And I've opened up a wide range of
possibilities for using different yarns

00:05:47.265 --> 00:05:48.375
with my knitting machine.

